=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">The RF model works perfectly at=
 detection. The system fails at </span><strong><span class=3D"">triage</spa=
n></strong><span class=3D"">. This is why large enterprises invest more in =
alert correlation engines and SOAR platforms than in detection model improv=
ement.</span></p><hr><h2><span class=3D"">What Large Enterprises Actually D=
o Differently</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"=
">They don't replace RF with deep learning. They layer:</span></p><p class=
=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><strong><span class=3D"">More signal sources</sp=
an></strong><span class=3D"">, not just packet capture. They add endpoint l=
ogs, proxy logs, authentication logs, cloud API logs, threat intelligence f=
eeds. The model fuses multiple evidence streams.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-m=
arkdown-paragraph"><strong><span class=3D"">Graph-based detection</span></s=
trong><span class=3D""> for lateral movement and privilege escalation. RF o=
n flow features misses the attack path across multiple hosts. They build id=
entity graphs and traffic graphs to catch the attack narrative.</span></p><=
p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><strong><span class=3D"">Alert aggregatio=
n and correlation</span></strong><span class=3D""> so 100,000 individual fl=
ow alerts become 15 incident cases. RF flags the individual suspicious flow=
s; a rules engine or another model groups them.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-ma=
rkdown-paragraph"><strong><span class=3D"">Continuous retraining pipelines<=
/span></strong><span class=3D""> where threat hunters label detection misse=
s, and models retrain weekly or daily, not once per project.</span></p><p c=
lass=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><strong><span class=3D"">Human-in-the-loop</=
span></strong><span class=3D""> for verdict. The model suggests; the analys=
t decides. This feedback loop is more valuable than squeezing another perce=
nt of model accuracy.</span></p><hr><h2><span class=3D"">The Actual Scaling=
 Path for Your Architecture</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><=
span class=3D"">Version 1 (what you're building now): Single RF model, sing=
le server, offline. This is correct for a first deployment.</span></p><p cl=
ass=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">If this expands to a large e=
nterprise later:</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=
=3D"">You'd keep the RF for per-flow classification. You'd add separate mod=
els for different detection perspectives. You'd add a correlation layer. Th=
e RF at the core remains unchanged because it does its job well.</span></p>=
<p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">You'd replace it only i=
f there's a detection gap you cannot close with feature engineering. Even t=
hen, you'd likely supplement rather than replace =E2=80=94 running RF and a=
 specialized model in parallel, merging their outputs.</span></p><hr><h2><s=
pan class=3D"">The Honest Summary</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragr=
aph"><span class=3D"">Random Forest handles large-scale flow classification=
 fine. The challenges that force large enterprises toward more complex arch=
itectures have nothing to do with model accuracy and everything to do with =
data diversity, alert management, and multi-signal correlation.</span></p><=
p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">Your current approach of=
 CicFlowMeter + RF + behavioral features scales to enterprise levels on the=
 detection side. The operational scaling =E2=80=94 training pipelines, per-=
segment models, alert triage =E2=80=94 would be added around it, not in pla=
ce of it.</span></p></div><div class=3D"dbe8cf4a">This response is AI-gener=
