></div><div class=3D"_4f9bf79 _43c05b5" data-virtual-list-item-key=3D"14" s=
tyle=3D"--assistant-last-padding-bottom: 24px;"><div class=3D"ds-message _6=
3c77b1" style=3D"--panel-width: 0px;"><div class=3D"ds-markdown ds-assistan=
t-message-main-content"><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D""=
>Good. Let me isolate exactly why CNN is a poor fit for your specific setup=
, point by point.</span></p><hr><h2><span class=3D"">1. Your Input Data Is =
Already Highly Processed</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><spa=
n class=3D"">CicFlowMeter output is a table of statistical summaries: mean =
packet size, flow duration, packet count ratios, flag counts. These are agg=
regated descriptors, not raw data.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragr=
aph"><span class=3D"">CNNs are designed to learn hierarchical spatial patte=
rns from raw or near-raw input =E2=80=94 pixels in images, samples in audio=
 waveforms, bytes in a payload. The convolution operation slides across adj=
acent values expecting local correlations that build into higher-level patt=
erns.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">In a Ci=
cFlowMeter CSV, adjacent columns have no spatial relationship. Column 7 (Fl=
ow Duration) next to Column 8 (Total Fwd Packets) is an arbitrary ordering.=
 Swapping their positions changes nothing about the meaning, but it complet=
ely changes what a convolutional filter sees. The convolution operation is =
mathematically meaningless on this data structure.</span></p><hr><h2><span =
class=3D"">2. The Features Are Already Human-Engineered</span></h2><p class=
=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">CicFlowMeter already did the ha=
rd work. It converted raw packet sequences into meaningful statistical feat=
ures: standard deviations, means, maxima, minima, ratios.</span></p><p clas=
s=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">The point of a CNN is to learn=
 features automatically when you don't know what features to extract. You a=
lready have the features. You're not skipping the feature engineering step =
by using a CNN; you're applying a feature learner to already-engineered fea=
tures. There's nothing left for the CNN to discover that isn't already expr=
essed in the numbers.</span></p><hr><h2><span class=3D"">3. The Signal Is i=
n Feature Values, Not Feature Structure</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-=
paragraph"><span class=3D"">Your detection logic relies on thresholds and r=
elationships like: "If flow duration is short but packet count is high and =
SYN flags dominate, it's a SYN flood."</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-pa=
ragraph"><span class=3D"">This is a decision boundary problem. Random Fores=
t excels at learning axis-aligned and oblique decision boundaries in featur=
e space. Each tree split is essentially asking "Is feature X above threshol=
d Y?" =E2=80=94 exactly the logic of intrusion detection rules.</span></p><=
p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">A CNN would try to find =
patterns across the feature vector as if it were a sequence or grid. But th=
e signal isn't in the sequential arrangement of features; it's in their val=
ues and simple combinations. The CNN is searching for a type of pattern tha=
t doesn't exist in your data representation.</span></p><hr><h2><span class=
=3D"">4. Behavioral Detection Doesn't Require Sequence Modeling at the Flow=
 Level</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">This =
is subtle but important. You mentioned behavioral detection as the main cha=
llenge.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">True =
behavioral detection would mean analyzing how traffic patterns evolve over =
time =E2=80=94 a server gradually increasing outbound data, periodic beacon=
ing to a C2 server, lateral movement across multiple hosts.</span></p><p cl=
ass=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">But CicFlowMeter outputs are=
 individual flow summaries. Each row is a completed or timed-out flow, not =
a moment in an ongoing sequence. You've lost the temporal resolution needed=
 for true sequence modeling.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><=
span class=3D"">You can simulate behavioral detection by aggregating multip=
le flows into time windows and computing statistical features =E2=80=94 and=
 that can work well. But that's feature engineering again, not sequence lea=
rning. A CNN or LSTM trained directly on flow sequences from CicFlowMeter i=
sn't seeing a true temporal signal; it's seeing a series of statistical sna=
pshots that already collapsed time internally.</span></p><hr><h2><span clas=
s=3D"">5. The Class Imbalance Problem Is Harder for Neural Networks</span><=
/h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">CIC-IDS2017 has sev=
ere class imbalance. Normal traffic dominates, some attack types have very =
few samples.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">=
Random Forest handles this gracefully with class weighting, balanced subsam=
pling, or adjusting the decision threshold after training. The tree structu=
re naturally separates sparse classes if the features are discriminative.</=
span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">CNNs require c=
areful batch balancing, loss function weighting, and often still collapse t=
oward the majority class during training. You'd spend more time fighting tr=
aining instability than improving detection.</span></p><hr><h2><span class=
=3D"">6. Deployment Complexity for No Gain</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdo=
wn-paragraph"><span class=3D"">Your system runs on an offline Windows serve=
r with no GPU. You need to:</span></p><ul><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-parag=
raph"><span class=3D"">Load the model</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-mar=
kdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">Process CSV files</span></p></li><li><p c=
lass=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">Output predictions</span></=
p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">Keep everyth=
ing maintainable by someone who admits limited ML knowledge</span></p></li>=
</ul><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">A trained Random F=
orest is a file you load with joblib. Inference is a few matrix multiplicat=
ions. You can open the model and inspect feature importances to debug false=
 positives.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">A=
 CNN requires managing TensorFlow or PyTorch dependencies on an offline mac=
hine, ensuring correct input reshaping, handling batch versus single infere=
nce modes, and debugging why an alert fired is far harder.</span></p><p cla=
ss=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">All this operational overhead=
 is acceptable if the model provides a genuine detection advantage. It does=
n't. You'd be paying complexity costs for identical or worse results.</span=
></p><hr><h2><span class=3D"">7. The Research Papers That Use CNNs on This =
Data Are Misleading</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span cla=
ss=3D"">Many academic papers report high CNN accuracy on CIC-IDS2017. They =
typically:</span></p><ul><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=
=3D"">Use the same dataset for training and testing without proper temporal=
 separation, so the model memorizes attack patterns rather than generalizes=
</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">Bal=
ance classes artificially before evaluation, hiding the false positive prob=
lem</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">=
Convert flow features into pseudo-images by arbitrary reshaping, creating s=
patial relationships that don't exist in reality</span></p></li><li><p clas=
s=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">Never deploy the model in a re=
al environment to measure actual false positive rates</span></p></li></ul><=
p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">These papers optimize fo=
r publication, not production. The reported accuracy doesn't translate to y=
our use case.</span></p><hr><h2><span class=3D"">The Core Mismatch in One S=
entence</span></h2><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span class=3D"">CNNs=
 learn spatial hierarchies from unstructured or semi-structured data. Your =
data is already structured, statistical, and low-dimensional. There's no hi=
erarchy to learn, no spatial structure to exploit, and no feature discovery=
 left to do. You're using a tool designed for a different problem entirely.=
</span></p></div></div><div class=3D"ds-flex _0a3d93b" style=3D"align-items=
